## 1. What is localStorage?

`localStorage` allows the browser to store data that remains available even after refreshing or reopening the webpage.

### Store data

```js
localStorage.setItem("name", "Abhishek");
```

`"name"` is the **key** and `"Abhishek"` is the **value**.

### Get data

```js
let name = localStorage.getItem("name");

console.log(name);
```

This retrieves the value stored under the `"name"` key.

### Remove data

```js
localStorage.removeItem("name");
```

This removes the data stored under the `"name"` key.

> **Important:** `removeItem()` takes the **key**, not the value.

---

## 2. Complete Example

This example demonstrates the basic operations of `localStorage`:

```js
let input = document.querySelector("#name");
let save = document.querySelector("#save");
let show = document.querySelector("#show");
let remove = document.querySelector("#remove");
let h2 = document.querySelector("h2");

// Save data
save.addEventListener("click", () => {
  localStorage.setItem("name", input.value);
});

// Retrieve data
show.addEventListener("click", () => {
  let name = localStorage.getItem("name");

  h2.innerText = name;
});

// Remove data
remove.addEventListener("click", () => {
  localStorage.removeItem("name");

  h2.innerText = "";
});
```

---

## 3. Understand the Flow

### Save

```text
User enters name
       ↓
input.value
       ↓
localStorage.setItem()
       ↓
Browser stores the data
```

### Show

```text
localStorage
       ↓
getItem("name")
       ↓
Retrieve stored value
       ↓
Display it
```

### Remove

```text
removeItem("name")
       ↓
Delete the stored data
       ↓
Clear the displayed name
```

---

## 4. The Four Methods You Must Remember

```js
localStorage.setItem("key", "value");  // Store
localStorage.getItem("key");           // Retrieve
localStorage.removeItem("key");        // Remove one item
localStorage.clear();                  // Remove everything
```

### Remember the difference

```js
localStorage.setItem("name", "Abhishek");
                         ↑       ↑
                        key    value
```

Therefore:

```js
localStorage.getItem("name");
localStorage.removeItem("name");
```

Both use `"name"` because `"name"` is the **key**.

